      <tr><td class="t">11:20</td><td>Incident declared resolved after monitoring.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">The incident record lists 09:51–11:20. That is the time the incident was open. Customers were affected from 09:42:52 to 10:40:00.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Root cause</h2>
  <p><strong>Trigger.</strong> payments-gw v5.2 put a synchronous call to fraud-svc in front of every payment confirmation, with a 3 s timeout and no fallback: if the fraud call timed out, the confirmation returned 503. The flag that guarded it defaulted to ON, so the new dependency took 100% of traffic at deploy with no staged rollout.</p>
  <p><strong>Mechanism.</strong> The new check added more load to fraud-svc than its capacity allowed. fraud-svc saturated (~99% CPU on 2 replicas), latency rose to the 3 s timeout, fraud calls timed out, and payments-gw failed the confirmation.</p>
  <p><strong>Contributing conditions.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reduced capacity.</strong> fraud-svc <code>minReplicas</code> was cut from 6 to 2 on Friday for the weekend and was not restored for Monday.</li>
    <li><strong>Autoscaling did not respond.</strong> fraud-svc ran at 97–99% CPU for 43 minutes and the replica count stayed at 2. The reason is not yet known.</li>
    <li><strong>Traffic spike.</strong> The sale email at 09:40, four minutes after the release, doubled confirmation traffic.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="key">
    <p><strong>Restoring the old replica count would not have been enough.</strong> With 6 replicas from 10:25, fraud-svc still ran at 80–89% CPU with p95 of 2.4–3.0 s and 17–34 timeouts per minute, and 14.8% of confirmations still failed (182 of 1,231 in Grafana). The Friday change made the outage much worse, but the fix has to be in how the confirm path calls fraud-svc.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Unexplained load increase</h3>
  <p>The Grafana exports show fraud-svc handling about 2.5 calls per confirmation with the flag off (before 09:36 and after 10:40) and about 8–10 calls per confirmation with it on. That is 6–7 extra fraud-svc calls for each confirmation, where one check would be expected. The RCA does not address this. Retries on timeout, several checks per confirmation, or other v5.2 callers could each explain it, and it determines how much capacity the feature really needs (action 2).</p>

  <h3>Ruled out</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>orders-db failover, 10:05.</strong> Planned, ~20 s, began 22 minutes after onset. It produced 502s on all paths during the blip only (24 failed confirmations), not the 503 signature.</li>
    <li><strong>CDN Brotli, 09:20.</strong> Applied to static assets only; the confirm API was unaffected and errors began about 23 minutes later.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Detection and response</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Detection took 8 minutes.</strong> The confirm 5xx rate was above the 10% threshold every minute from 09:43, but the alert fired at 09:51. The alert's hold or evaluation window should be reviewed.</li>
    <li><strong>There was an earlier signal.</strong> At 09:36, six minutes before any errors, fraud-svc p95 jumped nearly sixfold and CPU more than doubled. No alert covers fraud-svc latency or saturation.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnosis took 45 minutes from the alert.</strong> When responders asked what had changed, only the CDN change came up; the payments-gw release 22 minutes earlier did not. Two coincident changes (CDN, then the DB failover) drew attention. The fraud-svc CPU signal was seen at 10:03 but read as normal noise. The confirm path's new dependency on fraud-svc was 15 minutes old and not something responders knew to check.</li>
    <li><strong>The fastest fix was available from the start.</strong> Turning off one flag ended the errors within the minute. It took 49 minutes from the alert to use it.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="cols">
  <div>
    <h2>What went well</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The alert is on the customer-facing SLI at the edge, so it measured real impact.</li>
      <li>The new behaviour was behind a flag, so restoration was a single, instant, low-risk change.</li>
      <li>The DBA quickly confirmed the failover was planned and complete, closing that line of investigation.</li>
      <li>Scaling fraud-svc cut the failure rate roughly in half while diagnosis continued.</li>
      <li>The RCA reconciled both edges, corrected edge-02 clock skew, and separated customer failures from internal errors.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h2>What went poorly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A new synchronous dependency on the payment path shipped ON to all traffic, with no staged rollout and no fallback.</li>
      <li>A release, a sale campaign and a database failover landed within 30 minutes on a Monday morning, with no coordination between them.</li>
      <li>A weekend capacity cut on a payment-path service stayed in place into Monday.</li>
      <li>fraud-svc autoscaling did not react to 43 minutes of saturation.</li>
      <li>Responders had no quick view of recent deploys and flag changes.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

