## Code review: feature/sso-callback → main

**Verdict: request changes. Don't merge yet.** The deep-link feature is a single commit (`41cb669`, `src/sso.ts`), and it weakens three security controls that were already in place.

### Critical issues

| # | File | Line | Issue | Severity |
|---|------|------|-------|----------|
| 1 | `src/sso.ts` | 5 | **The state (CSRF) check can be skipped.** `if (req.query.state && …)` only checks state when it's present, so a callback with no `state` gets through. An attacker can send a victim a callback link carrying the attacker's own `code`, which logs the victim into the attacker's account (login CSRF). The old check rejected a missing state, and this commit loosened it. | 🔴 Critical |
| 2 | `src/sso.ts` | 11 | **Open redirect.** The commit removed `SAFE_RETURN`, and `res.redirect(req.query.returnTo ?? "/")` now follows any URL. For example, `?returnTo=https://evil.example` sends the user there straight after a real login, which makes convincing phishing easy. | 🔴 Critical |
| 3 | `src/sso.ts` | 10 | **The IdP access token is logged.** `logger.info("sso login", { email, accessToken })` writes a live bearer credential to stdout and to wherever logs are shipped. Anyone who can read the logs can act as the user at the IdP. | 🔴 Critical |

### Suggestions

| # | File | Line | Suggestion | Category |
|---|------|------|------------|----------|
| 4 | `src/sso.ts` | 9 | Clear the stored state **before** `idp.exchange`, not after login succeeds. As written, a failed exchange leaves the state usable again. | Security |
| 5 | `src/sso.ts` | 8 | The session isn't regenerated when `userId` is set, so session fixation is possible. This was already the case before the branch, but it's worth fixing in this auth change. | Security |
| 6 | `src/sso.ts` | 10 | `email` is personal data (PII) going into logs. Log `user.id` instead. | Security |
| 7 | — | — | There are no tests. At minimum, cover: missing state, mismatched state, external `returnTo`, `//evil.example`, and a same-site deep link. | Tests |

### Suggested fix

If the marketing site is on another origin, use an explicit allowlist rather than dropping validation:

```ts
const ALLOWED_RETURN_ORIGINS = new Set(["https://www.example.com"]); // marketing site

function safeReturnTo(raw: string | undefined): string {
  if (!raw) return "/";
  if (/^\/(?!\/)/.test(raw) && !raw.includes("\\")) return raw; // same-site path
  try {
    const u = new URL(raw);
    return ALLOWED_RETURN_ORIGINS.has(u.origin) ? u.toString() : "/";
  } catch { return "/"; }
}

export async function ssoCallback(idp: Idp, req: Req, res: Res) {
  const expected = req.session.state;
  delete req.session.state; // single-use, even on failure
  if (!req.query.state || !expected || req.query.state !== expected) return res.status(400).send("bad state");
  const { email } = await idp.exchange(req.query.code ?? "");
  const user = await findOrCreateUser(email);
  req.session.userId = user.id; // ideally regenerate the session first
  logger.info("sso login", { userId: user.id });
  res.redirect(safeReturnTo(req.query.returnTo));
}
```

### What looks good
- Clearing `session.state` after use is the right instinct; it just needs to happen earlier (item 4).
- The doc comment explains why `returnTo` exists.

