## Code Review: feature/sso-callback (41cb669)

**Request changes.** The commit is supposed to add deep links and clear state after use. Along the way it introduces three security regressions in `src/sso.ts`: the CSRF check can be bypassed, any `returnTo` URL is accepted, and access tokens are written to the logs.

### Critical Issues
| # | File | Line | Issue | Severity |
|---|------|------|-------|----------|
| 1 | src/sso.ts | 6 | **The state/CSRF check can be skipped.** The condition changed from `!state \|\| state !== session.state` to `state && state !== session.state`, so a callback with no `state` parameter now passes. An attacker can send a victim to `/sso/callback?code=<attacker's code>` and the victim ends up logged in as the attacker (login CSRF). Restore the old check. Also fail if `req.session.state` itself is missing. | 🔴 Critical |
| 2 | src/sso.ts | 11 | **Open redirect.** `SAFE_RETURN` was removed and `res.redirect(req.query.returnTo ?? "/")` follows any URL. That means `?returnTo=https://evil.example` sends users off-site straight after a trusted login, which is a phishing vector. Deep links only need same-site paths, so put the check back (see suggestion 1). | 🔴 Critical |
| 3 | src/sso.ts | 10 | **The access token is logged.** `logger.info("sso login", { email, accessToken })` writes a live bearer credential, as plain JSON, to stdout and on to whatever collects the logs. Remove `accessToken` from the log call. If it was ever deployed, rotate the affected tokens. | 🔴 Critical |

### Suggestions
| # | File | Line | Suggestion | Category |
|---|------|------|------------|----------|
| 1 | src/sso.ts | 3 | When you restore `SAFE_RETURN`, make it stricter. `^\/(?!\/)` still lets `/\evil.example` through, and browsers turn that into `//evil.example`. Use something like `/^\/(?![\/\\])/` and also reject control characters, or parse the value with `new URL(returnTo, origin)` and check that `.origin === origin`. | Security |
| 2 | src/sso.ts | 9 | Clear `state` before you call `idp.exchange` (right after it's validated). As written, an exchange that throws leaves the old state in the session, where it can be reused. | Correctness |
| 3 | src/sso.ts | 8 | The session ID isn't regenerated on login, so session fixation is possible. This was already true on main, but now is a good time to fix it. | Security |
| 4 | src/sso.ts | 10 | `email` is PII. Consider logging `user.id` instead. | Security |
| 5 | — | — | There are no tests. Add cases for: missing state, mismatched state, an off-site `returnTo`, a `/\host` `returnTo`, and checking that state is cleared after use. | Test coverage |

### What Looks Good
- Clearing `state` once it's used is the right fix for replay; it just needs to happen earlier (suggestion 2).
- The diff is small and focused, and the doc comment explains why `returnTo` exists.

### Verdict
**Request Changes.** Issues 1–3 block the merge. A minimal fix:

```ts
const SAFE_RETURN = /^\/(?![\/\\])/; // same-site paths only

