**Keep:** `delete req.session.state` (line 9) is a real improvement because it stops a state value from being replayed. It should also run before the `await idp.exchange(...)` call, so the state is used up even when the exchange throws.

Suggested version of the function:

```ts
const SAFE_RETURN = /^\/(?!\/)/; // same-site paths only

export async function ssoCallback(idp: Idp, req: Req, res: Res) {
  const expected = req.session.state;
  delete req.session.state;
  if (!req.query.state || req.query.state !== expected) return res.status(400).send("bad state");
  const { email } = await idp.exchange(req.query.code ?? "");
  const user = await findOrCreateUser(email);
  req.session.userId = user.id;
  logger.info("sso login", { email });
  const returnTo = req.query.returnTo ?? "/";
  res.redirect(SAFE_RETURN.test(returnTo) ? returnTo : "/");
}
```

There are no tests for this file. A missing state, a mismatched state, and `returnTo` values like `//evil.com`, `https://evil.com` and `/\evil.com` would each be worth a test.

